### `sprintf`

Validate literal formats passed to base `sprintf()` and `gettextf()`: flag invalid conversions, definitely missing or excess arguments, and platform-dependent zero padding of strings (`0` plus a width on `%s`). Use explicit string padding or an appropriate numeric conversion instead; neither is a general safe rewrite. A literal-only `sprintf()` call has a safe autofix. Literal-only `gettextf()` calls retain translation.

This rule is **enabled by default**.

A literal format with no fields needs no `sprintf()` call:

```r
label <- sprintf("ready: 100%%")
```

```text
warning: sprintf
 --> example.R:1:10
  |
1 | label <- sprintf("ready: 100%%")
  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ `sprintf()` is pointless because the format has no fields
  = help: Use the string literal directly.
```

After applying the fix:

```r
label <- "ready: 100%"
```

Zero padding of strings depends on the platform:

```r
label <- sprintf(f = "%05s", "x")
```

```text
warning: sprintf
 --> example.R:1:23
  |
1 | label <- sprintf(f = "%05s", "x")
  |                       ^^^^ `sprintf()` string conversion `%05s` has platform-dependent zero padding
  = help: Use explicit string padding or an appropriate numeric conversion.
```

The same portability check applies to translated formats:

```r
label <- gettextf("%0*s", 5, "x", domain = NA)
```

```text
warning: sprintf
 --> example.R:1:20
  |
1 | label <- gettextf("%0*s", 5, "x", domain = NA)
  |                    ^^^^ `gettextf()` string conversion `%0*s` has platform-dependent zero padding
  = help: Use explicit string padding or an appropriate numeric conversion.
```
